// Codata: negative types introduced by how they answer observations (ADR-66).
//
// A `data` block says what a value *contains* -- constructors and their field
// tuples. A `codata` block is the exact dual: it says what a value *answers* --
// named *observations*. This is the CBPV-negative half the substrate always
// reserved (values ARE; computations DO -- and a codata value answers demands).
//
// `Point` declares two observations, `x` and `y`, each answering an `Integer`.
// (An observation's result is a demand, so a value-typed `x: Integer` reads as
// the computation `x: F Integer`.)
codata Point { x: Integer, y: Integer }

// A *copattern definition* introduces a `Point` by saying, clause by clause,
// how it responds to each observation. The clauses lead with `.observation`,
// the dual of a constructor pattern. This definition is non-recursive -- a
// finite record of observations -- so it needs no fixpoint (corecursion, the
// productivity ladder, is a separate rung: wyrd-c2g4).
//
// Under the hood this is pure elaboration with ZERO frozen-core spend
// (ADR-66 route (a)): the copattern clauses compile through one `Cosplit`
// case-tree node to a record of graded thunks
//     #{ x = thunk_ω (ret 3), y = thunk_ω (ret 4) }
// over the ADR-45 record former. Each observation body is delayed by the
// existing `U_ω` thunk; nothing new is added to the core.
def rec origin() -> Point {
  .x => 3,
  .y => 4
}

// Observing is the eliminator, dual to constructing. `origin.x` projects the
// `x` field and forces its thunk -- ADR-66 §3.1's `s.π ↦ force(RecordProj(s, π))`.
// Here we observe BOTH `x` and `y` and add them: each observation independently
// forces its own delayed body, so the sum is `3 + 4`.
origin.x + origin.y

// The harness checks:
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-last-value: 7
